      <h2 id="about-title">Hi, I&rsquo;m <em>Elan.</em></h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>My name, pronounced &ldquo;uh-LAWN,&rdquo; means &ldquo;oak tree&rdquo; in Hebrew, which inspired the name Liminal Oak.</p>
        <p>I work with people carrying complex trauma, navigating addiction, or trying to make sense of a psychedelic experience. Many are deep thinkers who have reached the edges of what talking alone has offered them. Our work may also touch on moral injury, questions of meaning and purpose, and how to live with experiences that have challenged deeply held beliefs.</p>
        <p>I bring warmth, reflection, and curiosity to our sessions. I listen to what you say and help you attend to what is happening as you say it. We might spend time with a sensation, give a protective part of you a voice, or stay with an emotion that hasn&rsquo;t had much room.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="credentials">
        <p class="eyebrow">Background &amp; training</p>
        <p>MSW &amp; BS in Psychology · University of Washington</p>
        <p>Licensed Social Worker Associate – Independent Clinical<br /><span class="small-note">License SWIA.SC.61683693</span></p>
        <p>I have worked in the psychedelic field for over five years, including research at the <a href="https://ntap.psychiatry.uw.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Washington</a>. This work includes a study investigating psilocybin for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and alcohol use disorder (AUD) in military veterans and first responders.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
